  "content": {
    "id": "p50-q7-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Show that $3x+y=6$ is tangent to the circle $x^2+y^2+4x+16y+28=0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\Delta=(-8)^2-4(1)(16)=0$, so there is exactly one point of intersection and the line is tangent to the circle.",
      "worked": "Same method as above. Substitute $y=-3x+6$ into the circle equation.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+(-3x+6)^2+4x+16(-3x+6)+28&=0\\\\x^2+9x^2-36x+36+4x-48x+96+28&=0\\\\10x^2-80x+160&=0\\\\x^2-8x+16&=0\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=b^2-4ac\\\\&=(-8)^2-4(1)(16)\\\\&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThere is exactly one point of intersection, hence the line is tangent to the circle.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
